{"name":"Muddy River","hex":"#715d3d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"muddy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Covered or splashed with, or full of, mud (“wet soil”).","Of water or some other liquid: containing mud or  other sediment in suspension; cloudy, turbid."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or splash (someone or something) with mud.","To cover or splash (someone or something) with mud."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/muddy"},{"term":"river","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large and often winding stream which drains a land mass, carrying water down from higher areas to a lower point, oftentimes ending in another body of water, such as an ocean or in an inland sea.","Any large flow of a liquid in a single body."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To improve one’s hand to beat another player on the final card in a poker game."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/river"}],"entries":[{"title":"Muddy River (Massachusetts)","blocks":[{"text":"The Muddy River is a series of brooks and ponds that runs through sections of Boston's Emerald Necklace, including along the south boundary of Brookline, Massachusetts. The river is a protected public recreation area surrounded by parks and hiking trails, managed by the Massachusetts Department of Conservation and Recreation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Olmsted-Park-Muddy-River.jpg/330px-Olmsted-Park-Muddy-River.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muddy_River_(Massachusetts)","wikidata":"Q1951716"},{"title":"Muddy River (Merrymeeting Bay)","blocks":[{"text":"The Muddy River is a 4.7-mile-long (7.6 km) river in Topsham, Maine, which empties into Merrymeeting Bay. The river is known to be very muddy."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muddy_River_(Merrymeeting_Bay)","wikidata":"Q14715307"},{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River","wikidata":"Q4022"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Back_Bay_Fens_from_the_Forsyth_Way_footbridge%2C_November_2019.jpg/500px-Back_Bay_Fens_from_the_Forsyth_Way_footbridge%2C_November_2019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Back_Bay_Fens_from_the_Forsyth_Way_footbridge%2C_November_2019.jpg/1280px-Back_Bay_Fens_from_the_Forsyth_Way_footbridge%2C_November_2019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Back_Bay_Fens_from_the_Forsyth_Way_footbridge%2C_November_2019.jpg/500px-Back_Bay_Fens_from_the_Forsyth_Way_footbridge%2C_November_2019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Muddy River in the Back Bay Fens","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABack_Bay_Fens_from_the_Forsyth_Way_footbridge%2C_November_2019.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimed